    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Soledad's origins began with Mission Nuestra Señora de la Soledad founded by the Spanish in 1791 under Padre Fermín de Lasuén.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soledad%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1872 the Southern Pacific Railroad granted right‑of‑way, establishing a station that turned Soledad into an agricultural hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soledad%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town grew around Rancho San Vicente, where Catalina Munrás subdivided land in the 1860s, donating plots for a school, church, and cemetery.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soledad%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Soledad hosts the restored Mission, a key landmark that attracts visitors and reflects the town’s early religious heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soledad%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town lies in Steinbeck Country; John Steinbeck’s novel \"Of Mice and Men\" is set near Soledad, linking literature to the valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soledad%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Pinnacles National Park borders Soledad, offering hikers dramatic rock formations and a glimpse of the region’s unique geology.",
